1: # P3K.27. Proved: primitive scalar families and their actual rotating Cox normal framing
2: 
3: For the ten primitive scalar families of K22, use the actual
4: positive scalar loop, the standard vertical-edge cusp, and the
5: normal product transported by the extending cusp origin. Its
6: free normal-framing gauge agrees with the transported rotating
7: framing of K9. A possible constant rotation loop extends over
8: the fixed three-disc and changes neither integer. Thus the
9: rotating characteristic pair and the corresponding relative
10: coordinates may be taken to be
11: \[
12:        k=-3d,\qquad \ell=-24,\qquad
13:        (m_{\rm rel},n_{\rm rel},s)
14:              =(6,\ 3d-6,\ 1-d/2\bmod28).
15:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.1}
16: \]
17: Here \(d=8,14,20,30\) is the height denominator in K22. The
18: coordinate convention is exactly K23, including its complex
19: orientation and the positively oriented Eells--Kuiper generator.
20: This does not identify a further edge's relative four-cycle.
21: 
22: ## 1. The actual two products and their literal transition
23: 
24: Write the actual periods as
25: \[
26:  h:(Z,V)\mapsto(qZ,\alpha(q)V),\qquad
27:  \gamma:(Z,V)\mapsto(g(q)Z,q\lambda\beta(q)V).
28: \]
29: On the positive parameter circle, \(|\lambda|\) is fixed.
30: After quotienting the physical delta circle, the parameter
31: phase in this gamma period disappears: the orbit period is
32: \((Z,|V|)\mapsto(g(q)Z,|q\lambda\beta(q)|\,|V|)\).
33: The same assertion holds in each actual Cox chart: its
34: parameter phase has the physical-delta weights, so it is the
35: identity on that circle orbit. The chosen fan and its
36: period-cell quotient therefore supply a fixed smooth orbit
37: body along this parameter circle, before applying the
38: closing translation.
39: 
40: The actual ordinary closing translation is \(q^{-1}\) in the
41: line character. Returning its period cell by gamma leaves
42: the bounded unit map \((g,\lambda\beta)\). It is removed by
43: the actual extending cusp origin
44: \[
45:  \sigma(v)=(\Gamma v,Cv+(v^2-v)/2),\qquad
46:  \lambda=e^{2\pi iv},\quad
47:  g=e^{2\pi i\Gamma},\quad\beta=e^{2\pi iC}.
48:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.2}
49: \]
50: Indeed
51: \[
52:  \sigma(v+1)-\sigma(v)=\gamma-s\delta,\qquad
53:              s=\frac{\log q}{2\pi i}.
54: \]
55: Thus the ordinary deck minus the cusp-origin difference is
56: the actual negative gamma period. In the period quotient
57: it is the identity. This proves the closed cusp product,
58: including the representative-cell identification; it is
59: not a choice of a new parameter loop.
60: 
61: On the same universal parameter interval the bounded
62: coordinate change is
63: \[
64:  C_\theta=
65:  \left(g^\theta,\,
66:        (\beta\lambda_0)^\theta
67:                        e^{\pi i\theta(\theta-1)}\right).
68:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.3}
69: \]
70: All powers use the given logarithms. These are literal
71: bounded torus maps and extend through both adjacent
72: unimodular charts. They also extend in the Cox master
73: coordinates used in K23. At \(\theta=1\) they equal the
74: bounded period-return map, as required. The last factor
75: is physical delta and acts trivially on the orbit normal
76: three-plane. Thus the product from (P3K.27.2) is precisely
77: the normal product obtained by carrying the Cox charts
78: through their actual bounded clutch.
79: 
80: The ordinary extending origin is
81: \[
82:                   o=(-\Gamma s,-Cs-s^2/2-sv).
83: \]
84: The actual cusp-to-ordinary transition in the two product
85: coordinates is \(\sigma-o\). Its angular change is
86: \[
87:         \theta\gamma+\frac{\theta(\theta-1)}2\delta.
88:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.4}
89: \]
90: On the free orbit annulus, the second term is the identity
91: and the first is the marked gamma translation. In the
92: source-preserving Seifert product of P3F.9, that translation
93: is exactly \(R_\theta\), rotation of its gamma circle.
94: This retains the source-translation trivialization
95: \(Z\mapsto\exp(t\log g(q))Z\) used there.
96: 
97: ## 2. The real extension at the node and its derivative
98: 
99: The radial part of (P3K.27.4) contains \(|q|^\theta\).
100: It is used as an annular expression only. The actual smooth
101: extension in the orbit is the following fixed product map,
102: which agrees with that annular expression.
103: 
104: Use P3F.9's two exact small-resolution charts for
105: \(xy=uv\):
106: \[
107:  (x,v,z),\ u=xz,\ y=vz;\qquad
108:  (u,y,w),\ x=uw,\ v=yw.
109: \]
110: The physical circle has weights \((-1,+1)\) on \((v,z)\)
111: in the first chart, and \((+1,-1)\) on \((u,w)\) in the
112: second. Its actual normal Hopf coordinates are
113: \[
114:  (y,b),\quad b=(|z|^2-|v|^2)/2;
115:  \qquad
116:  (x,b'),\quad b'=(|u|^2-|w|^2)/2.
117:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.5}
118: \]
119: The fixed curves in these charts are \(v=z=0\) and
120: \(u=w=0\), respectively. On their overlap, with \(r=|z|^2\),
121: \[
122:  2b=r-|y|^2/r,\qquad
123:  2b'=|x|^2r-r^{-1},\qquad
124:  \frac{db'}{db}
125:        =\frac{|x|^2+r^{-2}}{1+|y|^2r^{-2}}>0.
126:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.6}
127: \]
128: Choose the relative, increasing proper fibre coordinate
129: \(\chi(x,y,b)\) constructed in P3F.9, agreeing with the
130: marked gamma coordinate on the outside collar. Its
131: derivative \(\chi_b\) is strictly positive. It gives the
132: fixed smooth map
133: \[
134:          \Phi:(x,y,b)\longmapsto(x,y,\chi(x,y,b))
135: \]
136: to the nodal source neighbourhood times the gamma circle,
137: with the corresponding second-chart formula.
138: The construction is independent of the parameter angle
139: in the closed cusp product (P3K.27.2).
140: 
141: The actual quotient family, in the two extending products,
142: is glued on its outside annulus by (P3K.27.4). It therefore
143: has the following explicit smooth description in the
144: constant orbit:
145: \[
146:           J_\theta=R_\theta\circ\Phi
147:              \quad\hbox{on its cusp piece},             \tag{P3K.27.7}
148: \]
149: with the fixed ordinary product on its other piece.
150: Equation (P3K.27.4) proves that these maps agree on the
151: actual annular identification. Equation (P3K.27.6) proves
152: smoothness through the two node charts. Thus (P3K.27.7)
153: is an actual comparison map of the orbit family, rather
154: than a formal differentiation of a fractional monomial
155: at \(q=0\).
156: 
157: At the first fixed chart, quotient the derivative by the
158: tangent to its fixed curve, including the derivative of
159: its graph gamma coordinate. On the normal coordinates
160: \((\Re y,\Im y,b)\), the resulting map is
161: \[
162:  Q_x=
163:  \begin{pmatrix}
164:  1&0&0\\
165:  0&1&0\\
166:  \chi_{\Re y}&\chi_{\Im y}&\chi_b
167:  \end{pmatrix},\qquad \chi_b>0.                         \tag{P3K.27.8}
168: \]
169: There is the same matrix in the second chart with \(x,b'\)
170: in place of \(y,b\). These are the actual normal derivatives
171: at the node endpoints as well: (P3K.27.6) has no excluded
172: nonzero-source-coordinate hypothesis in either smooth
173: Hopf chart.
174: 
175: The matrices (P3K.27.8) are independent of \(\theta\).
176: Their off-diagonal entries contract linearly to zero,
177: and their last diagonal entry contracts through positive
178: numbers to one. The overlap derivative in (P3K.27.6) is
179: positive, so these contractions are the coordinate
180: expressions of the global normal-bundle comparison.
181: Intrinsically, the free gamma vector gives its oriented
182: normal line, and the quotient is the normal two-plane
183: bundle of the immersed nodal normalization in the source.
184: The derivative is the identity on that quotient and is
185: positive on the gamma line. Choosing a splitting is
186: contractible; the preceding contractions therefore glue
187: globally even though the source normal two-plane bundle
188: has nonzero Euler class.
189: The source tangent shear, already removed in obtaining
190: (P3K.27.8), also contracts through invertible triangular
191: matrices. In particular the \(|q|^\theta\) annular factor
192: introduces no omitted normal derivative: it has been
193: extended by the explicitly smooth map (P3K.27.7).
194: 
195: ## 3. The fixed-frame comparison
196: 
197: The initial Cox product is explicit on the whole fixed
198: sphere. Write its two affine coordinates as
199: \(\zeta,\zeta'=\zeta^{-1}\), and its two complex normal
200: coordinates as \(c_0,c_1\), with
201: \(c_i'=\zeta c_i\). In the quaternion convention of K23 put
202: \[
203: \begin{aligned}
204:  q_{\rm H}
205:    &=(1+j\zeta)(c_0+j\overline{c_1})\\
206:    &=(\zeta^{-1}+j)(c_0'+j\overline{c_1'}).
207: \end{aligned}                                          \tag{P3K.27.10}
208: \]
209: These are literally equal. To check the overlap, substitute
210: \(c_i'=\zeta c_i\) and use
211: \(zj=j\bar z\) for a complex number \(z\).
212: The first product expands as
213: \[
214:        c_0+j\bar c_1+j\zeta c_0-\bar\zeta\bar c_1,
215: \]
216: and the second has exactly these four terms.
217: The formula is real linear and invertible on every normal
218: fibre, including either chart origin. It therefore gives
219: the global real normal product, without an unnamed
220: trivialization of \(\mathcal O(-1)^{\oplus2}\).
221: 
222: The physical weights \((-1,+1)\) give
223: \(q_{\rm H}\mapsto q_{\rm H}e^{-i\theta}\).
224: Consequently its global Hopf normal coordinate is
225: \(q_{\rm H}i\overline{q_{\rm H}}\).
226: For \(y_{\rm H}=c_0c_1\) and
227: \(b_{\rm H}=(|c_1|^2-|c_0|^2)/2\), the inner Hopf vector is
228: \[
229:  (c_0+j\bar c_1)i(\bar c_0-j\bar c_1)
230:    =-2b_{\rm H}i+2\Im(y_{\rm H})j-2\Re(y_{\rm H})k.
231:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.11}
232: \]
233: Conjugation by \(1+j\zeta\) gives the desired global
234: three-plane frame. The same overlap identity proves that
235: the second-chart expression agrees. The local ordered
236: coordinates \((\Re y_{\rm H},\Im y_{\rm H},b_{\rm H})\)
237: have the opposite orientation to the positive physical
238: Hopf normal orientation, as retained in K23; the displayed
239: matrix has negative determinant in those local coordinates
240: and hence preserves the prescribed normal orientation.
241: 
242: Choose the global positive-Hopf normal frame supplied by
243: this actual Cox quaternion product at parameter zero.
244: Push it to the constant orbit by \(\Phi\). K9 chooses
245: exactly such a fixed initial frame and transports it by
246: the actual \(R_\theta\) flow. By (P3K.27.7), the Cox
247: frame in the closed cusp product is carried to that same
248: frame:
249: \[
250:         dJ_\theta(\text{Cox frame})
251:            =dR_\theta\,d\Phi(\text{initial Cox frame}).
252:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.9}
253: \]
254: The derivative comparison in (P3K.27.8) is independent
255: of the parameter and cannot contribute a three-dimensional
256: normal-framing class on \(F\times S^1_\theta\).
257: Changing the initial frame changes a map
258: \(S^2\to SO(3)\), which is null-homotopic; it extends
259: over the fixed three-disc and is transported by the same
260: flow. This retains the actual initial Cox marking.
261: 
262: For completeness, the bounded coordinate maps in
263: (P3K.27.3) are applied to both products before this
264: comparison. Their positive parts cancel as the same
265: normal-bundle isomorphism. Their unitary part in the
266: K23 quaternion coordinate \(q_{\rm H}\) is
267: \[
268:                    q_{\rm H}\longmapsto
269:                          a_\theta q_{\rm H}b_\theta,
270: \]
271: with \(a_\theta,b_\theta\) independent of the fixed-curve
272: point. Here is the literal bounded unitary identity fixing
273: this assertion. Write the first Cox chart as
274: \(q=\zeta c_0c_1\). A unitary torus map has the form
275: \[
276:   (\zeta,c_0,c_1)\longmapsto
277:                   (a\zeta,p c_0,q_1c_1),\qquad apq_1=1.
278: \]
279: Choose its continuous square roots along the parameter interval,
280: \(A^2=pq_1\), \(B^2=p/q_1\), with \(AB=p\) initially
281: and hence throughout. Substitution in (P3K.27.10) gives
282: \[
283:                       q_{\rm H}\longmapsto A q_{\rm H}B.
284:                                                         \tag{P3K.27.12}
285: \]
286: For example the two complex coefficients of \(q_{\rm H}\),
287: in the form \(a_{\rm H}+jb_{\rm H}\), are
288: \(c_0-\bar\zeta\bar c_1\) and
289: \(\bar c_1+\zeta c_0\). They are multiplied by \(AB\)
290: and \(B/A\), respectively, on both sides of
291: (P3K.27.12). This proves the identity without an implicit
292: normal-frame convention. The induced Hopf normal map is
293: \(\eta\mapsto A\eta\bar A\), a constant
294: three-dimensional rotation. Dependence of \(g,\beta\)
295: on the base produces only the source tangent shears:
296: in the fixed normal charts the base monomial is
297: quadratic in the two complex normal variables, so its
298: higher unit terms have zero normal linear part.
299: Any residual constant rotation loop is the
300: \(\mathbb Z/2\) summand of
301: \(\pi_1{\rm Map}(S^2,SO(3))\); it extends over the fixed
302: three-disc and contributes neither \(k\) nor \(\ell\).
303: There is no free \(\pi_3(SO(3))\) discrepancy between
304: the two actual framings.
305: 
306: ## 4. The integer coordinates
307: 
308: We include the complete degree-one normal-clutch calculation in the actual matched framing.
309: 
310: P3K.22 identifies the page as the original rational elliptic
311: source minus a regular fibre, with its original line \(M\).
312: The page boundary has binding degree one along the positive
313: inward gamma circle. The actual K6 graph boundary is
314: \(\gamma=\theta+g_\epsilon(p)\), where \(p\in T^2\) and
315: \(\theta\) is the positive physical scalar-parameter direction.
316: The cap-to-ordinary arrow and the two extending origins in K22.3
317: give exactly this positive direction; no parameter cover is used.
318: 
319: Use the graph normal consisting of its oriented normal in the
320: orbit \(Y\) and the two Cartesian parameter directions.
321: Use on the compression the actual rank-three normal frame
322: transported by \(R_\theta\). The compression is the same solid
323: torus minus a ball as in K6. Its normal bundle has \(w_2=0\):
324: its tangent bundle is trivial, and \(TY\oplus\mathbb R\) is
325: trivial for the actual orbit \(Y=S^5\). Obstruction theory on
326: this three-dimensional compression gives a normal framing.
327: Its restriction at the fixed-sphere end differs from the
328: positive-Hopf frame by a map \(S^2\to SO(3)\), which extends
329: over the fixed \(D^3\). Transport that extension by the same
330: \(R_\theta\). This defines the rotating compression-cap
